A foolproof Dutch oven method for tender, juicy pulled pork. The spiced brown sugar dry rub permeates the meat overnight, followed by a hard stovetop sear and a gentle 5- to 6-hour oven braise.

*Adapted from [Martha Stewart](https://www.marthastewart.com/956163/braised-pulled-pork-shoulder).*

## Ingredients

- 8 to 10 pound bone-in Boston butt pork shoulder
- 3 tablespoons packed light-brown sugar
- 2 tablespoons coarse kosher salt
- 1 teaspoon freshly ground black pepper
- 2 teaspoons paprika
- ½ teaspoon dry mustard
- ½ teaspoon ground cumin
- ½ teaspoon cayenne pepper
- 2 tablespoons vegetable oil
- 2 cups water (or chicken broth)
- Barbecue sauce, for serving
- Soft potato buns or brioche rolls, for serving

## Instructions

1. **Dry Rub and Rest:** Place the pork shoulder on a large rimmed baking sheet. In a small bowl, whisk together the brown sugar, coarse salt, black pepper, paprika, dry mustard, cumin, and cayenne. Generously rub the spice mixture across all sides of the pork. Cover tightly with plastic wrap and refrigerate for at least 2 hours, or ideally overnight.
2. **Bring to Temp and Sear:** Preheat oven to 325°F (165°C). Remove the pork from the refrigerator and let rest at room temperature for 30 minutes. Pat dry with paper towels. Heat a large heavy Dutch oven over medium-high heat and add the vegetable oil. Add the pork shoulder and sear, turning with tongs, until deeply golden brown on all sides (about 4 minutes per side).
3. **Slow Braise:** Pour 2 cups of water into the bottom of the Dutch oven around the meat. Position the pork shoulder fat-side up. Cover tightly with the lid and transfer to the oven. Cook for 5 to 6 hours, uncovering briefly to baste with pan juices every hour, until a meat fork twists effortlessly into the center.
4. **Shred and Serve:** Transfer the pork shoulder to a large cutting board. Remove the bone (it should slide right out) and any excess fat cap. Shred the meat into bite-sized strands using two forks. Moisten with a few spoonfuls of the degreased cooking juices from the Dutch oven, then serve warm on potato buns with barbecue sauce.

## Notes

- **Make-Ahead:** Pulled pork reheats wonderfully. Store shredded meat in the refrigerator submerged in its strained braising juices; reheat gently on the stovetop or in a slow cooker.
